<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Micah (prophet)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">late 8th century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century-bce">8th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Micah was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> prophet from Moresheth in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shephelah">Shephelah</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judah">Judah</a>, active in the late 8th century BCE under the kings Jotham, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahaz">Ahaz</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hezekiah">Hezekiah</a>. The Book of Micah, one of the twelve minor prophets, condemns the oppression of the poor and foretells the destruction of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samaria">Samaria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>. Its call to do justice, love mercy and walk humbly with God is among the best-known lines of prophetic ethics.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/micah-prophet.jpg" alt="Micah (prophet)" width="320" height="426" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 18 century icon painter · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Micah_prophet.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
